  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They never led. Not once. Victor Bericoto hit a solo shot for show, but the Dodgers seized the first inning and the Giants chased the entire night, stranding runners in pairs while the deficit grew. Two in the second, three more in the fifth and sixth, and by the time anyone got warm in the bullpen, the game was already decided. Eight hits and eleven strikeouts is the shape of a lineup that showed up at the wrong moment. Yunior Marte lasted only 4.2 innings, six runs on eight hits. The starter couldn't hold what the bats couldn't build on anyway. Duncan Davitt closed it out clean, but closing a 4\u201310 loss is not a save.",
    "what_this_means": "They had their chances and failed at them. Sixteen runners reached base in scoring position. Two converted. That's the math. Left fourteen runners stranded across innings two and eight when they needed to match what Los Angeles was doing early and often. Bericoto's two RBI mattered, but they came too late and too small against a team that didn't let this game stay close past the midpoint. This is inconsistency dressed as bad luck: they show you flashes (a home run, eight hits total) and nothing comes together at scale. The pitching was not the problem. The offense was not the problem. Both were problems at the same time, and that's how six-run margins get built. Thirty-one games back and sinking is what happens when you leave this many runners on base.",
    "what_to_watch": "LAD starts TBD tonight at 1:10 PT. Matt Wilkinson takes the ball for the Giants on a day when getting ahead matters more than chasing.",
